    "summary": "Mixed‑energy show with a blazing Ship of Fools solo and strong Space",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers the highlights – an iconic Ship Of Fools solo, an engaging Eyes → Drums → Space sequence, and a solid Black Peter. The first set drifts, with uneven energy and a weak Victim performance. Vince’s piano on Big River adds a jazzy lift.",
    "review": "Richfield Coliseum on June 9‑1992 was a night of highs and lows for the Dead. While the opening set never quite found its footing – “Victim Or The Crime” fell flat and the band sounded distracted – the second half sparked back to life. Jerry’s incendiary solo on Ship Of Fools has been called the best ever, even as his final Eyes solo was muffled by PA problems. The Eyes → Drums → Space trek built a soaring momentum that culminated in an inspired Space where he slipped in a wistful “Oh Shenandoah” riff. Vince’s piano on Big River floated with a jazzy swagger, and the band closed strong with Black Peter into Turn On Your Lovelight before a gentle Brokedown Palace encore.\n\nThe recordings reflect this mixed picture: the Schoeps‑AKG SBEOK capture (gd92-06-09.schoeps-akg.stevens.12574.sbeok.flacf) offers a fairly balanced mix that lets the standout moments shine, though the PA issue on Eyes remains audible. The Miller SBD sources add similar praise for Ship Of Fools and the Eyes‑Drums‑Space suite but note first‑set sound hiccups. For listeners seeking the clearest window onto this uneven night, the Schoeps‑AKG recording is the recommended pick.",
